    "commonPrompt": "Use case: stylized-concept.\nAsset type: finished single-item sprite for the Unity 2D sandbox game FlatWorld.\nPrimary request: redraw the spear in the supplied original reference as a coherent large-cluster pixel-art game weapon.\nInput images: image 1 is the original weapon and defines material identity and functional parts ONLY; image 2, the current brown bear, defines the required CHUNKY pixel-cluster scale and low-detail art language; image 3 is the 32x48 player and defines game readability.\nComposition: ONE complete isolated spear, straight horizontal, wooden butt at LEFT and sharp spear tip pointing RIGHT (+X); centerline exactly horizontal, entire weapon fully visible with generous transparent padding on all sides. Wide landscape canvas, ideally 1536x512, weapon occupies about 90% of width. Shaft roughly three quarters of overall length; head plus fastening roughly one quarter. No perspective shortening, no diagonal pose.\nStyle: genuinely coarse retro pixel art with very large hard-edged square pixel clusters, as if authored on roughly a 96x32 logical grid and displayed large. Make each shaft about 3 logical pixels thick, head about 9 logical pixels tall. Simple natural proportions, muted colors, one broad light face and one broad shadow face, sparse chunky highlights, clean thin deep-brown outline. The full-resolution pixels must remain sharp. Do not add details just because the canvas is large.\nConstruction: a long slightly uneven warm brown wooden pole; clearly readable tan plant-fiber cord wrapping and a simple crossing tie fastening the head at the actual head-to-pole joint. The cord is load-bearing attachment, not only decoration on the grip. Simple early handmade construction; no modern standardized socket, screws or machined ferrule.\nBackdrop: real transparent alpha background, no scene, no floor, no cast shadow, no checkerboard drawn into the image.\nAvoid: antialiasing, blur, painted textures, smooth gradients, 3D, tiny scratches, dense wood grain, busy surface facets, noise, glow, black heavy outlines, extra weapons, hands, labels, logo or watermark.",
    "assets": [
      {
        "id": "Spear_Stone",
        "promptSuffix": "Subject: STONE SPEAR. A gray chipped-stone leaf-shaped head with a slightly irregular silhouette and only 2 or 3 broad matte slate-gray facets, clearly primitive flaked stone, NOT metal. Warm natural wooden shaft; beige plant-fiber ties firmly crossing around the stone head base. Keep this a long thin thrusting spear, not a short knife, paddle or axe."
      },
      {
        "id": "Spear_Copper",
        "promptSuffix": "Subject: COPPER SPEAR. A simple elongated pointed leaf-shaped rudimentary hand-forged COPPER head. Distinct muted reddish copper orange, russet shadow, one broad dull orange-lit face; slight handmade asymmetry, few planes. This is copper, NOT gold, brass or bronze, no green patina. Beige fiber cord securely lashes its tapered tang to the warm brown wooden pole."
      },
      {
        "id": "Spear_Iron",
        "promptSuffix": "Subject: WROUGHT-IRON SPEAR. A simple elongated forged iron leaf-shaped blade with a central broad ridge, dark charcoal blue-gray shadow and muted silver-gray upper facet. Simple believable hand-forged head, slight asymmetry, matte metal with one restrained broad highlight, no polished bright white outline, not chrome. Tan fiber/rough leather wraps visibly bind the tapered tang to the dark warm brown wooden pole."
      },
      {
        "id": "Spear_Obsidian",
        "promptSuffix": "Subject: OBSIDIAN SPEAR. Preserve the black-purple volcanic-glass head and brown wood plus tan-fiber lashing identity from image 1, but make much larger and simpler pixel clusters than that original. Sharp knapped leaf-shaped head, irregular flaked edges, just 3 broad near-black violet-gray facets and one sparse muted lilac edge highlight. Black glass, NOT gray stone and NOT steel. NO glow, NO magic. Use the same long wooden-pole proportions as a simple stone spear, and draw the cord firmly securing the head. IMPORTANT reverse the original leftward orientation: finished sharp tip points RIGHT, butt LEFT."
      }
    ]
  },
